<v NARRATOR>From Spark and Anvil and DialogueQuest — this is Dash, in chapter 1 of the DialogueQuest cast.

2
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:16.101
<v NARRATOR>This drama uses an AI-generated voice. Voiced via Gemini 2.5.

3
00:00:16.101 --> 00:00:23.272
<v NARRATOR>Dash is a zippy little chipmunk, practically vibrating with energy on a busy afternoon.

4
00:00:23.272 --> 00:00:27.963
<v NARRATOR>The moment Patter began — I was wondering if you—

5
00:00:27.963 --> 00:00:32.454
<v DASH>—if you keep the interruptions? Yes! That's me!

6
00:00:32.454 --> 00:00:41.265
<v NARRATOR>His speech bubble had crashed right into the end of Patter's, with a little dash where the two met, like two train cars coupling.

7
00:00:41.265 --> 00:00:54.356
<v DASH>Sorry. My name is Dash. I keep the interruptions. When someone's too excited, or too worried, they jump in — they cut the other person off. That mark right there, the dash? That's me.

8
00:00:54.356 --> 00:01:06.647
<v NARRATOR>He showed how two calm, polite creatures could suddenly catch fire — finishing each other's lines, tumbling over one another.

9
00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:08.258
<v DASH>But if we leave now—

10
00:01:08.258 --> 00:01:10.669
<v DASH>—we'll miss it, I know, that's why—

11
00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:20.600
<v DASH>—we have to run! See the dashes? Every one is a place someone couldn't wait. It makes the talk feel fast. Alive.

12
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:33.011
<v NARRATOR>Then he showed the other side — two creatures interrupting *every single line,* over and over, until it stopped feeling exciting.

13
00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:40.862
<v DASH>Too much. If everybody always interrupts, it's just noise. The dash only works when it's a surprise.

14
00:01:40.862 --> 00:01:52.393
<v NARRATOR>Patter felt the click — his young writers never interrupted; every line waited politely, so their talks felt slow and stiff.

15
00:01:52.393 --> 00:02:03.004
<v DASH>Find the most exciting moment. The part where someone's bursting to speak. End their line with a dash — and let the next character crash right in.

16
00:02:03.004 --> 00:02:16.215
<v DASH>But only *sometimes.* An interruption is like a loud noise. One makes you jump; a hundred and you stop hearing them. The polite turns are what make the interruptions *matter.*

17
00:02:16.215 --> 00:02:20.906
<v NARRATOR>A young writer asked what an interruption shows about a character.

18
00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:33.957
<v DASH>Feeling. Big feeling. Nobody interrupts when they're calm. So when your character cuts someone off, the reader feels it — *this one can't hold it in anymore.*

19
00:02:33.957 --> 00:02:41.528
<v NARRATOR>Afterward, Dash finally wound down, still for a real moment, no dashes.

20
00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:55.779
<v DASH>I used to think interrupting meant I was rude. But it's not that I don't care — it's that I care so *much.* The interrupting isn't me not listening. It's me caring out loud, too fast to wait.

21
